legacy.descriptor:
{
      "description": "Set an account's display name (id = stable tag). Blank name \u2192 422.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "properties": {
          "account_id": {
            "description": "Which account to give a display name to. NOTHING checks that the account exists - the id goes straight into an alias row and the call answers 200 echoing back the id you sent. A misspelled id therefore writes a perfectly valid-looking alias that will never join to anything: the name you set simply never appears, and the response gave you no way to tell. Copy the id from a monitoring read rather than typing it.",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "display_name": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "type": "string"
              },
              {
                "type": "null"
              }
            ],
            "default": null,
            "title": "Display Name"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "account_id"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "type": "object"
      },
      "name": "update_account"
    }

{
      "description": "Ingest an agent's context gauge (in-memory; bad body \u2192 400). Answers with a bounded receipt (``agent_id``, ``ts``), not the stored entry echoed back \u2014 call ``get_monitoring`` when you need the rest.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "properties": {
          "compaction_count": {
            "default": null,
            "description": "Optional non-negative live Codex compaction count. Identity remains the verified JWT sub.",
            "title": "Compaction Count"
          },
          "context_pct": {
            "default": null,
            "description": "How full this session's context window is, on a 0-100 PERCENT scale. The only check is that it is a JSON number - anything else is a flat 400 - with no range check at all, and the value is stored exactly as sent. The reply is a bounded receipt (agent_id, ts), not the stored entry echoed back, so nothing in it repeats the number you sent; call get_monitoring when you need to read the gauge back. The scale is the whole point: this is the number the server's context-high band compares against the owner's PAIR of offboard points — notice_pct (the soft notice) and handover_pct (the final one, and where the handover fires), both whole percents. Reporting 0.85 for 'eighty-five percent' therefore sits below both and neither the notice nor the handover ever fires, while reporting 500 is above both and they fire at once. Both answer 200 and are indistinguishable in the response.",
            "title": "Context Pct"
          },
          "rate_limits": {
            "additionalProperties": true,
            "description": "Free-form rate-limit gauge for this session. Its keys and values are copied into the store as they arrive - nothing is validated, so a misspelt key is kept just as faithfully as a correct one. The trap is the word MERGE: this call merges onto the session's previous entry, but rate_limits is the exception and is overwritten on EVERY call, so a report that omits it replaces whatever you last sent with an empty object and answers 200. Send the whole gauge every time, or the previous one is gone.",
            "title": "Rate Limits",
            "type": "object"
          }
        },
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "type": "object"
      },
      "name": "ingest_agent_context"
    }

{
      "description": "Read one block of the boot context by kind/key, folded (the owner's edit ⊕ the shipped seed). Carries size_chars/cap_chars so an edit can be sized before it is made, is_default/has_seed to tell an edited block from the shipped one, and read_only for the blocks that are shown but may never be edited. An unknown kind or key is a 404 that names the keys that exist.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "properties": {
          "key": {
            "description": "The second half of the address. Most kinds serve exactly one key; a kind that serves more does so because the documents genuinely differ, so the key is never a formality — an unknown one is a 404 that names the keys that exist.",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "kind": {
            "description": "The first half of the address, and one of the values this ENUM lists — also the document-history kind this block's retained versions are filed under. The enum IS the list: a kind outside it is not served, and a kind that ships is added to it in the same change, so there is nothing to look up and nothing to go stale.",
            "enum": [
              "system_interaction",
              "boot_sequence",
              "offboard",
              "accelerated_stop",
              "task_closeout",
              "task_reassign_predecessor",
              "task_takeover_with_predecessor",
              "task_takeover_fresh",
              "task_unblocked",
              "task_ready_for_done"
            ],
            "type": "string"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "kind",
          "key"
        ],
        "type": "object"
      },
      "name": "get_boot_doc"
    }

{
      "description": "Replace the EDITABLE HALF of one boot-context block ({kind, key, body}) — text every agent reads at boot, or is sent when a lifecycle event happens to it. body is REQUIRED and unknown keys are rejected; emptying a body that had content needs allow_shrink=true. The stored result is judged against that block's own cap. A read-only block refuses with 405 for every caller. The read-only head is NOT sent and cannot be: the server joins the shipped head back on, so no caller has any way to write it. Owner or admin assistant only. Answers with a bounded receipt (``kind``, ``key``, ``is_default``, ``size_chars``, ``cap_chars``, ``sha256``), not the document — call ``get_boot_doc`` when you need the rest.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "properties": {
          "allow_shrink": {
            "type": "boolean"
          },
          "body": {
            "description": "The ENTIRE editable half, not an addition to it. Sending a fragment answers 200 and permanently replaces the rest of the body, so read the current body with get_boot_doc first and fold your change into it. The read-only head is not part of this and cannot be sent — the server joins the shipped one back on — so the only thing you can lose here is your own half. The guards catch only the extremes: emptying a body that had content needs allow_shrink, the character cap is enforced on the stored result, and a read-only block refuses outright. A {name} in braces is refused ONLY on the blocks that declare variables — the ones that declare none (the 系統互動 block quotes JSON in its body) store braces as typed and every agent reads them that way, so this is not a net. Losing most of the body is none of those: it passes without comment, and every agent that reads this block afterwards reads the shortened text as though it were the whole thing.",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "key": {
            "description": "The second half of the address. Most kinds serve exactly one key; a kind that serves more does so because the documents genuinely differ, so the key is never a formality — an unknown one is a 404 that names the keys that exist.",
            "type": "string"
          },
          "kind": {
            "description": "The first half of the address, and one of the values this ENUM lists — also the document-history kind this block's retained versions are filed under. The enum IS the list: a kind outside it is not served, and a kind that ships is added to it in the same change, so there is nothing to look up and nothing to go stale.",
            "enum": [
              "system_interaction",
              "boot_sequence",
              "offboard",
              "accelerated_stop",
              "task_closeout",
              "task_reassign_predecessor",
              "task_takeover_with_predecessor",
              "task_takeover_fresh",
              "task_unblocked",
              "task_ready_for_done"
            ],
            "type": "string"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "kind",
          "key",
          "body"
        ],
        "type": "object"
      },
      "name": "replace_boot_doc"
    }

{
